---
import ServiceLayout from '../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';

const hero = { src: '/img/services/firepit.jpg', alt: 'Stone patio and circular fire feature overlooking a green landscape', caption: 'Stonework creates a generous outdoor gathering room', width: 1024, height: 1024 };
const process = [
  { title: 'Define the use', copy: 'Dining, gathering, circulation, grade changes and views call for different dimensions and relationships. We begin with what the space needs to make possible.' },
  { title: 'Fit the site', copy: 'Existing architecture, trees, slope, drainage and access shape the layout. The hardscape should feel anchored to the property rather than dropped onto it.' },
  { title: 'Select materials', copy: 'Color, texture, scale, durability and maintenance are considered together. Samples and project context are more useful than choosing from an isolated catalog image.' },
  { title: 'Prepare and build', copy: 'Excavation, base, edges, drainage and transitions receive the same attention as the visible surface. Planting then settles the structure into the garden.' },
];
const faqs = [
  { question: 'What does hardscaping include?', answer: 'Residential hardscaping can include patios, walkways, steps, retaining or seat walls, fire features, garden edging and other built elements. The appropriate scope depends on the property, intended use and any permit or engineering requirements.' },
  { question: 'Do you design the planting around the patio or wall?', answer: 'Yes. Garden & Art’s differentiator is treating structure and planting as one composition. Plants soften edges, frame views, provide shade or enclosure and keep an outdoor room connected to the larger garden.' },
  { question: 'Which patio material is best for Raleigh?', answer: 'There is no single best material. Natural stone, concrete pavers, brick and other options differ in appearance, cost, installation, heat, maintenance and how they relate to the house. Site conditions and the design direction should guide the choice.' },
  { question: 'Why do base preparation and drainage matter?', answer: 'The visible surface is only one part of a durable hardscape. Excavation depth, compacted base, edge restraint, slope and water movement help limit settlement, movement and puddling. Requirements vary with material, soil and use.' },
  { question: 'Can you build a retaining wall on a sloped yard?', answer: 'Many slopes can be shaped with landscape walls, but height, loading, drainage, property lines and local requirements affect what is appropriate. Walls that require engineering or permitting must be handled to those standards rather than treated as simple garden edging.' },
  { question: 'Can hardscape work be phased?', answer: 'Often, but the complete design and construction sequence should be understood first. It is usually inefficient to install planting or irrigation where future excavation, access or utility work will disturb it.' },
];
const related = [
  { href: '/landscape-design-raleigh/', title: 'Landscape design', copy: 'Start with a complete plan that connects outdoor rooms to the rest of the property.' },
  { href: '/landscape-lighting-raleigh/', title: 'Landscape lighting', copy: 'Plan subtle path, step and focal lighting before hardscape construction is complete.' },
  { href: '/drainage-rain-gardens-raleigh/', title: 'Drainage & rain gardens', copy: 'Address grading and water movement around patios, walls and walkways.' },
];
---

