    'A patio is not simply a paved rectangle. Its size affects whether chairs can move comfortably; its position changes the view from inside; its edges decide how the garden meets the gathering space. The same thinking applies to a path, wall, set of steps or fire feature. Each element should solve a practical need and strengthen the whole composition.',
    'Garden & Art combines design, horticulture and landscape construction under one point of view. Norman Rabins considers architecture, grade, water, mature trees and planting from the beginning. The result is hardscape that supports the way you want to live outdoors without feeling separate from the land around it.',
  ]}
  checklist={[
    'Patios for dining, gathering and everyday outdoor use',
    'Walkways, garden paths, steps and entry sequences',
    'Retaining walls, seat walls and terraced planting areas',
    'Fire features and focal elements for outdoor rooms',
    'Planting, lighting and drainage coordinated with construction',
  ]}
  processEyebrow="Design and construction"
  processTitle="What you see rests on"
  processEmphasis="what you do not."
  process={process}
  localEyebrow="A garden-plus-structure approach"
  localTitle="Hardscape should look"
  localEmphasis="better as the garden grows."
  localLede="The most convincing outdoor rooms balance durable materials with living edges, shade, texture and seasonal change."
  localParagraphs={[
    'We use planting to connect built work to the property: softening a wall, creating enclosure around a patio, framing a path or preserving the presence of a mature tree. Material joints, wall lines and path curves can echo the house without making the landscape feel rigid.',
    'Raleigh’s clay soil and heavy rain make water movement and base preparation central to the design. Patios need intentional slope; walls need appropriate drainage; runoff needs a route that does not erode adjacent beds. These details are not decorative, but handling them well allows the finished space to feel effortless.',
  ]}
  gallery={[
    { src: '/img/portfolio/14.jpg', alt: 'Large stone outdoor fireplace beside a flagstone patio', caption: 'A substantial stone focal point anchors the patio', width: 1000, height: 1333 },
    { src: '/img/portfolio/16.JPG', alt: 'Circular brick fire pit with curved stone garden seating', caption: 'A woodland gathering space shaped around the fire', width: 1000, height: 1333 },
  ]}
  factorsEyebrow="Materials, schedule and investment"
  factorsTitle="What shapes a hardscape"
  factorsEmphasis="project."
  factorsIntro="Pricing should follow a defined design and site review. Surface area matters, but access, excavation, walls, utilities and preparation can be just as important."
  factors={[
    { title: 'Layout and grade', copy: 'Curves, elevation changes, steps and retaining conditions affect excavation, layout time and the amount of structural work below the surface.' },
    { title: 'Material selection', copy: 'Natural stone, pavers and brick vary in unit cost, labor, pattern, waste and edge details. The right comparison considers the installed system.' },
    { title: 'Access and removal', copy: 'Equipment access, demolition, hauling and protection of the house, lawn and mature trees influence how the work can proceed.' },
    { title: 'Connected systems', copy: 'Drainage, sleeves, irrigation, electrical preparation, lighting and planting should be sequenced before surfaces close off access.' },
  ]}
